## Exceptions

# Why Exception, not BaseException, is the catch-root

. BaseException includes SystemExit/KeyboardInterrupt/GeneratorExit — control-flow
"stop" signals, not bugs.
. Catching broadly with Exception is safe; catching BaseException risks swallowing
Ctrl+C/exit signals.

# try/except/else/finally

. Only try is mandatory; add except/else/finally only when they serve a real purpose.
. else = runs only on success; keeps risky code separate from success-only code.
. finally = always runs, even through a pending return/break/continue.
. Execution order gotcha: a return inside try/except is held, finally runs first, then
the held value returns.
. Anti-pattern: return inside finally silently overrides pending returns AND silently
swallows exceptions that were about to propagate — never put control-flow in finally.

# Custom exceptions

. Subclass Exception. super().__init__(message) sets the string representation (str(e))
— skip it, str(e) shows a raw, unhelpful dump of constructor args instead.
. Custom attributes (self.age = age) are independent of the message mechanism —
plain instance-attribute assignment, same as any class; works whether or not
super().__init__() was called.
. repr() = debugging-oriented string (shows quotes/type); str() = human-readable.
Same underlying mechanism as __repr__/__str__ dunders from OOP.

# Exception chaining (raise X from Y)

. Wraps a low-level error in a higher-level, more meaningful one for callers — without
losing the original.
. from e is Python-native raise syntax — automatically sets __cause__ = e; never
assigned manually.
. Purpose: caller shouldn't need to know implementation-detail exception types;
original stays available via .__cause__ for deep debugging.
. raise X from None exists to explicitly suppress chaining (flagged, not practiced
this session).

# Re-raising (raise alone) vs. alternatives

. Returning an exception object as data = error-checking becomes optional/forgettable.
. Swallowing (except: pass) = failure invisible to caller entirely.
. Re-raise = do local work (log, cleanup) without hiding failure from caller —
guarantees visibility unless explicitly caught later.
. raise (no arg) preserves original traceback.

# Exception hierarchy & multi-except ordering

. Catching a parent class catches all its subclasses (except LookupError catches
KeyError+IndexError; except AppError catches ValidationError+DatabaseError).
. Hard rule: parent must come after its subclasses in except chain —
general-before-specific makes the specific handler unreachable dead code.
. Sibling exceptions (neither is subclass of the other) — order between them is
arbitrary/style only, not a correctness rule. Only parent-vs-child ordering is
mandatory.
. Catch specifically near the error's origin (different failures need different
responses); catch broadly (shared base) further up as a safety net — automatically
covers future subclasses without maintenance.
. except Exception: raise at the outer layer = distinguish "anticipated app error"
from "genuinely unexpected bug" — don't paper over real bugs.
. Bare except: (no exception type specified) catches everything, including
SystemExit/KeyboardInterrupt — always catch specific types, or at minimum except
Exception:, never a bare except:.

